/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - In a MOST network up to 64 devices are joined in a ring: each device receives from the one before and sends to the one after.
   - One device, usually the head unit, is the timing master.
   - At the application layer each device is described by function blocks (for example an amplifier block or a tuner block) that other devices control with short control messages.
   - A MOST25 frame is 64 bytes (512 bits): 1 byte of header (preamble and boundary descriptor), 60 bytes of data, 2 bytes of control data and 1 byte of trailer (frame control and parity).
   - R is the data rate of a channel in bit/s; f is the frame rate, equal to the system frequency (44 100 frames per second at 44.1 kHz).
   - MOST50 doubles the frame to 1024 bits and MOST150 makes it 3072 bits, about 150 Mbit/s at 48 kHz; MOST150 also adds a channel that carries Ethernet packets and one for isochronous video.
   - Two networks can be joined at different OSI layers.
   - The central gateway does six jobs:
   - A networked car of today is an example: a 500 kbit/s powertrain CAN (engine, gearbox), a chassis CAN or FlexRay (ABS and ESP, steering, suspension), a 125 kbit/s body CAN with LIN sub-buses (doors, seats, climate), a MOST ring or Ethernet f
*/
