/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Before OBD-II each maker had its own socket and its own codes.
   - The other pins are left to the maker.
   - The tool sends a request whose first byte is a service (or mode) number; the ECU answers with the service number plus 0x40.
   - A parameter identifier (PID) names one value.
   - OBD-II reaches only the emissions-related data.
   - A diagnostic trouble code (DTC) is a letter and four digits.
   - Monitors are self-tests the ECU runs: misfire, fuel system and comprehensive components run all the time; catalyst, oxygen sensor, oxygen sensor heater, evaporative system and EGR run only when their conditions are met during a drive cycle 
*/
